 "endonym": {
  "native": "الخلافة العباسية",
  "roman": "al-Khilafah al-Abbasiyyah"
 },
 "capsule": "The Abbasid Caliphate, proclaimed in 750 after the overthrow of the Umayyads and seated at Baghdad from that city's founding in 762, remained nominally the supreme Sunni religious-political authority across this five-century window even as its direct territorial rule contracted sharply after the ninth-century breakup into autonomous provinces such as the Tulunids, Samanids and, later, the Buyid amirates. Reduced from the mid-tenth century to a figurehead first under Buyid and then Seljuk overlords in Iraq itself, the caliphs nonetheless retained unique legitimizing authority, invested regional rulers with titles, and briefly recovered real political power under al-Nasir (r. 1180-1225). The caliphate's institutional continuity, running back to Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah, was finally extinguished when the Mongol Ilkhan Hulagu sacked Baghdad and executed the last Baghdad caliph, al-Musta'sim, in 1258.",
 "citations": [
